QB.add(19,[{"id":19001,"t":"Physiology and Pathophysiology","s":"Which statement best distinguishes physiology from pathophysiology?","o":["Physiology: mechanisms behind normal function; pathophysiology: those mechanisms in disease","Physiology: mechanisms behind disease; pathophysiology: those mechanisms in health","Physiology: the structure of organs; pathophysiology: the structure of diseased organs","Physiology: only cellular function; pathophysiology: only organ function","Physiology and pathophysiology both study molecules only"],"e":"<b>Physiology</b>: characteristics (<b>chemical, physical, mechanistic</b>) contributing to <b>function and the processes of life</b>; investigated at cellular, organ and whole-body levels. <b>Pathophysiology</b>: <b>pathology in the context of physiology</b> – a disease process or injury, i.e. how the mechanistic system is changed or damaged and how that contributes to disease. The line with cell and molecular biology is “murky”.","w":["Swapped.","Function, not structure.","Cell, organ and whole body.","More levels."],"k":"Physiology vs pathophysiology.","rn":[["","Pathophysiology","Pathophysiology","Pathology in physiology context; e.g. disease or injury"],["","What does pathophysiology ask about physiology?","What does pathophysiology ask about physiology?","How it is changed/damaged; How that causes disease"]]},{"id":19002,"t":"Physiology and Pathophysiology","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> a reason given for physiology mattering in medicine?","o":["It replaces the need for diagnostic tests","It affords effective treatment in disease","It allows monitoring of the patient","It grants avenues towards optimised and novel treatments","It helps discover novel disease seen first by clinicians"],"e":"Physiology: affords <b>effective treatment</b>, allows <b>monitoring</b>, grants avenues to <b>optimised and novel treatments</b>; the recording adds that novel diseases are usually seen <b>first by a clinician</b>, and treatments are becoming more molecular and targeted. The lecturer stressed you still need tests (e.g. ion imbalances).","w":["Given.","Given.","Given.","Given."],"k":"Physiology aids treatment, monitoring, innovation."},{"id":19003,"t":"Homeostasis","f":"pi09","s":"Which sequence correctly describes the negative feedback loop?","o":["Stimulus → sensing → controller → effector → back","Stimulus → controller → sensing → effector → back","Stimulus → effector → controller → sensing → back","Sensing → stimulus → effector → controller → back","Controller → effector → controller, with no sensing"],"e":"Negative feedback loop: <b>stimulus → sensing → controller → effector</b>. When a variable moves <b>beyond nominal</b>, the controller initiates negative feedback via the effector to return it to the <b>normal (nominal) range</b>. “Negative feedback means I'm always going to dampen down.” Most control systems act this way.","w":["Sensing first.","Effector last.","Stimulus first.","That is positive feedback."],"k":"Stimulus → sensing → controller → effector.","rn":[["","What are the 4 steps of the negative feedback loop?","What are the 4 steps of the negative feedback loop?","Stimulus; Sensing; Controller; Effector"]]},{"id":19004,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"A variable plotted over time wiggles but stays between two dashed lines. What do the dashed lines represent?","o":["The nominal range kept by homeostasis","The lethal limits of the variable","The thresholds for positive feedback","The resting membrane potential","The ion concentration range"],"e":"The dashed lines mark the <b>NOMINAL RANGE</b>; the variable (e.g. temperature, BP, blood pH) naturally changes a little but is kept within it around a set point. Temperature is a <b>range</b>, not one number (some people ~36–36.5 °C).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Dashed lines = nominal range.","rn":[["","Nominal range","Nominal range","Range around set point; Variable works correctly; Between upper and lower limits"],["","Image card: What type of feedback does this graph show? How can you tell?","What type of feedback does this graph show? How can you tell?","Negative feedback; Variable stays within nominal range; Changes are corrected back"]]},{"id":19005,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"Name the process of keeping defined internal conditions constant as the environment changes.","e":"Homeostasis uses negative feedback: stimulus → sensor → controller → effector → back.","k":"Homeostasis.","a":["homeostasis"],"rn":[["","Homeostasis","Homeostasis","Maintaining constant conditions; Despite a changing environment"]]},{"id":19006,"t":"Homeostasis","f":"pi10","s":"A student runs up a hill to a lecture and starts sweating. Which loop and outcome describes this?","o":["Loop B: too hot → cool down → sweat cools blood","Loop A: nominal → balance → no action → no sweat","Loop B: too hot → imbalance → warm up → shivering to raise heat","Positive feedback raising temperature further","The baroreceptor reflex lowering temperature"],"e":"<b>Loop A</b>: receptor detects nominal set temperature; balance; no action; no sweat. <b>Loop B</b>: receptor detects higher temperature (“too hot”); imbalance; action required (“cool down”); effector = <b>sweat</b>. Sweat <b>evaporates</b>, cooling the blood. A neural mechanism constantly monitors <b>core</b> temperature.","w":["Running heats.","Cool down.","Negative.","Different loop."],"k":"Too hot → sweat (loop B).","rn":[["","How does sweating cool you?","How does sweating cool you?","Sweat evaporates; Evaporation cools the blood"],["","Thermoregulation scenario B: what happens?","Thermoregulation scenario B: what happens?","Action required to maintain equilibrium; Effector: sweat; Cool down"]]},{"id":19007,"t":"Homeostasis","f":"pi11","s":"A patient's blood pressure suddenly rises. How does the baroreceptor reflex respond?","o":["Less vasomotor activity → slower heart, vasodilation","More vasomotor activity → faster heart, vasoconstriction","Less vasomotor activity → faster heart, vasoconstriction","More vasomotor activity → slower heart, vasodilation","No change, as baroreceptors respond only to low BP"],"e":"Baroreceptors in the <b>carotid</b> and <b>aortic arch</b> walls. <b>High BP</b>: <b>reduced activity</b> in the <b>vasomotor centre</b> → <b>decreased heart rate</b> and <b>vasodilation</b>. <b>Low BP</b>: relaxed baroreceptors → <b>increased</b> vasomotor activity → <b>vasoconstriction</b> and <b>increased heart rate</b>.","w":["Low BP response.","Opposite.","Mixed.","Both."],"k":"High BP → ↓HR + vasodilation.","rn":[["","High BP: what are the 2 responses?","High BP: what are the 2 responses?","Decreased heart rate; Vasodilation"],["","Low BP: what happens to vasomotor centre activity?","Low BP: what happens to vasomotor centre activity?","Increased activity"],["","High BP: what happens to vasomotor centre activity?","High BP: what happens to vasomotor centre activity?","Reduced activity"]]},{"id":19008,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"In the baroreceptor reflex, which structure is the controller and which the sensor?","o":["Controller: medulla vasomotor centres; sensor: baroreceptors","Controller: baroreceptors; sensor: medulla vasomotor centres","Controller: the heart; sensor: the blood vessels","Controller: sympathetic nerves; sensor: arterial pressure","Controller: the kidney; sensor: the lungs"],"e":"Reference set point → input to <b>brain medulla</b>; <b>controller</b>: medulla <b>vasomotor centres</b> (“a bunch of neurons that control this”); <b>effectors</b>: sympathetic nervous system → blood vessels, heart; <b>controlled variable</b>: arterial pressure; <b>sensor</b>: <b>baroreceptors</b>. A change from the set point is the error.","w":["Swapped.","Effectors.","Effector.","Not."],"k":"Medulla controls; baroreceptors sense.","rn":[["","Baroreceptor loop: what is the controller?","Baroreceptor loop: what is the controller?","Brain medulla vasomotor centres"]]},{"id":19009,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"A healthy student stands up quickly after lying down and briefly feels dizzy. What is the explanation?","o":["Baroreceptors responded too slowly; usually not pathological","Baroreceptors failed completely, which is always pathological","Positive feedback caused the heart to fail briefly","Sweating dropped the blood pressure","Hyperkalaemia caused an arrhythmia"],"e":"Getting up quickly and feeling dizzy (sometimes with spots) means the baroreceptor system has <b>not responded rapidly enough</b> to bring about vasoconstriction or a slight HR increase. This is <b>not pathological</b> unless it happens all the time. Adjustments are constant and tiny.","w":["Slow, not failed.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Postural dizziness = slow baroreflex.","rn":[["","Why are you dizzy standing up quickly?","Why are you dizzy standing up quickly?","Baroreceptors too slow; No time to constrict; Or raise heart rate"]]},{"id":19011,"t":"Homeostasis","f":"pi12","s":"Why do we not use positive feedback to maintain normal set points such as blood pressure?","o":["It amplifies without control, causing instability","It is too slow to respond to sudden changes in pressure","It needs sensors that the body lacks","It always lowers the variable too far","It only works in plants"],"e":"<b>Positive feedback</b>: controller → effector, a <b>self-perpetuating</b> cycle where the variable is <b>amplified but not controlled</b> → <b>instability</b>. Using it for BP: the minute it got high you'd make it higher – “that would be madness”. It is appropriate only in some circumstances.","w":["Amplifies.","Not.","Amplifies.","Not."],"k":"Positive feedback amplifies → instability."},{"id":19012,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"During labour, cervical stretch triggers release of a hormone that causes more contraction and more stretch. Name the hormone.","e":"Oxytocin → contraction → more stretch → more oxytocin: positive feedback.","k":"Oxytocin.","a":["oxytocin"],"rn":[["","Birth feedback: first 2 steps?","Birth feedback: first 2 steps?","Cervix stretch releases oxytocin; Oxytocin: uterus contracts"]]},{"id":19013,"t":"Homeostasis","f":"pi14","s":"A trauma patient loses 2 L of blood and is untreated. Why can this become fatal?","o":["Low pressure and coronary flow weaken the heart further","Low pressure triggers negative feedback that always restores it fully","High pressure and coronary flow overwork the heart","Sweating causes dehydration and collapse","Oxytocin release lowers blood pressure"],"e":"<b>1 L loss</b>: recoverable (vasoconstriction suffices). <b>2 L</b>: potentially non-recoverable – too little blood for the heart to pump effectively; arterial pressure falls, <b>coronary blood flow reduces</b>; the heart muscle gets less oxygen, pumps less; the cycle <b>amplifies</b> towards death. The heart is not “some magical muscle” – it needs blood too.","w":["Positive feedback cycle.","Falls.","Not.","Not."],"k":"2 L loss → positive feedback towards death.","rn":[["","Blood loss: how does the cycle amplify?","Blood loss: how does the cycle amplify?","Weaker heart, lower pressure; Less coronary flow; Even weaker heart"],["","Why does 2 L blood loss become a vicious cycle?","Why does 2 L blood loss become a vicious cycle?","Arterial pressure falls; Coronary flow reduces; Positive feedback amplifies"],["","Why does falling arterial pressure weaken the heart?","Why does falling arterial pressure weaken the heart?","Less oxygen, nutrients; So pumps less"]]},{"id":19014,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"Why can't the body rescue itself after very large blood loss?","o":["Only vasoconstriction and a faster heart can help, and both fail","Only the kidneys can help, and they stop working instantly","Only sweating can help, and it dehydrates the patient","The brain switches off homeostasis after large losses","Blood becomes acidic immediately and stops flowing"],"e":"There are only two ways to raise BP: <b>vasoconstriction</b> and making the <b>heart pump faster</b>. After too much loss, squeezing vessels cannot raise pressure enough, and the heart becomes <b>fatigued</b> from pumping faster (using oxygen) – “a mechanical problem, pump problem, oxygen problem”.","w":["Two ways.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Limited compensation after big blood loss.","rn":[["","What are the only 2 ways to increase blood pressure?","What are the only 2 ways to increase blood pressure?","Vasoconstriction; Make the heart pump faster"]]},{"id":19015,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"Which statement correctly summarises feedback?","o":["Negative maintains BP/temperature; positive can be good or harmful","Positive maintains BP/temperature; negative can be good or harmful","Negative causes death after blood loss; positive aids birth","Feedback is irrelevant to pathology","Only negative feedback exists in the body"],"e":"Recap: positive feedback can be <b>good (childbirth)</b> or <b>extremely detrimental (blood loss)</b>; negative feedback generally maintains temperature and BP; <b>pathology can be seen as disruption of homeostasis</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Positive.","Relevant.","Both."],"k":"Feedback summary.","rn":[["","Negative vs positive feedback: generally good or bad?","Negative vs positive feedback: generally good or bad?","Negative: generally good; Positive: generally bad, sometimes useful"],["","Positive feedback is harmful in which example?","Positive feedback is harmful in which example?","Blood loss"]]},{"id":19016,"t":"Fluid and Solutes of the Body","f":"pi17","s":"In a 70 kg adult with 42 L of body fluid, roughly how is it distributed?","o":["~28 L intracellular (2/3), ~14 L extracellular (1/3)","~14 L intracellular (1/3), ~28 L extracellular (2/3)","~21 L intracellular, ~21 L extracellular","~28 L intracellular, ~14 L plasma","~38 L intracellular, ~4 L extracellular"],"e":"Total body fluid <b>42 L</b>: <b>intracellular 28 L (66.7%)</b>; <b>extracellular 14 L (33.3%)</b> – plasma 2.8 L (6.7%), interstitial 10.4 L (24.7%), transcellular 0.8 L (1.9%). Learn the <b>ballpark</b>, not exact figures (they vary between textbooks). Memory aid: 2/3 : 1/3.","w":["Swapped.","Not equal.","ECF.","Not."],"k":"2/3 intracellular, 1/3 extracellular.","rn":[["","What is intracellular fluid volume?","What is intracellular fluid volume?","28 L; 66.7% of body fluid"],["","What is extracellular fluid volume?","What is extracellular fluid volume?","14 L; 33.3% of body fluid"]]},{"id":19017,"t":"Fluid and Solutes of the Body","s":"Cerebrospinal fluid belongs to which fluid compartment?","e":"CSF is transcellular fluid (part of the ECF).","k":"Transcellular.","a":["transcellular","transcellular fluid"],"rn":[["","What is transcellular fluid volume?","What is transcellular fluid volume?","0.8 L; 1.9% of body fluid"],["","Example of transcellular fluid?","Example of transcellular fluid?","Cerebrospinal fluid"],["","Transcellular fluid","Transcellular fluid","Between two membranes; Not in a cell"]]},{"id":19018,"t":"Fluid and Solutes of the Body","s":"Which membrane separates plasma from interstitial fluid, and which separates interstitial fluid from intracellular fluid?","o":["Capillary membrane; cell membrane","Cell membrane; capillary membrane","Basement membrane; nuclear membrane","Capillary membrane; basement membrane","Lymphatic wall; cell membrane"],"e":"Diagram (Guyton & Hall): the <b>capillary membrane</b> lies between plasma and interstitial fluid; the <b>cell membrane</b> between interstitial and intracellular fluid. Lymphatics return fluid to plasma; output via kidneys, lungs, faeces, sweat, skin.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Cell membrane.","Capillary."],"k":"Capillary vs cell membrane.","rn":[["","What separates plasma from interstitial fluid?","What separates plasma from interstitial fluid?","Capillary membrane"],["","What separates interstitial fluid from intracellular fluid?","What separates interstitial fluid from intracellular fluid?","Cell membrane"]]},{"id":19019,"t":"Fluid and Solutes of the Body","s":"Which of the following is a non-ionic solute?","o":["Glucose","Bicarbonate","Chloride","Magnesium","Phosphate"],"e":"Solutes: <b>ions</b> – cations (Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺, H⁺, Mg²⁺) and anions (OH⁻, Cl⁻, HCO₃⁻, SO₄²⁻, PO₄³⁻, charged proteins); and <b>non-ionic</b> solutes, e.g. <b>glucose</b>, proteins.","w":["Anion.","Anion.","Cation.","Anion."],"k":"Glucose = non-ionic solute.","rn":[["","What are 2 examples of non-ionic solutes?","What are 2 examples of non-ionic solutes?","Glucose; Proteins"]]},{"id":19020,"t":"Fluid and Solutes of the Body","s":"Which is the approximate blood sodium concentration given?","o":["145 mM","116 mM","29 mM","4 mM","1.8 mM"],"e":"Blood: <b>K⁺ 4</b>, <b>Na⁺ 145</b>, <b>Cl⁻ 116</b>, <b>HCO₃⁻ 29</b>, amino acids 9, <b>Ca 1.8</b> mM. Know the ballpark (“outside the cell there is more sodium, about 145 mM”).","w":["Cl⁻.","HCO₃⁻.","K⁺.","Ca."],"k":"Na⁺ ≈ 145 mM.","rn":[["","Sodium concentration in blood?","Sodium concentration in blood?","145 mM"]]},{"id":19021,"t":"Fluid and Solutes of the Body","f":"pi19","s":"Which ions dominate inside cells compared with outside?","o":["Inside: K⁺, Mg²⁺, phosphate, proteins; outside: Na⁺, Cl⁻, HCO₃⁻","Inside: Na⁺, Cl⁻, HCO₃⁻; outside: K⁺, Mg²⁺, phosphate, proteins","Inside: K⁺, Na⁺, proteins; outside: Cl⁻, HCO₃⁻, Mg²⁺","Inside: Ca²⁺ at mM levels; outside: K⁺","Equal concentrations inside and outside"],"e":"Intracellular: lots of <b>K⁺</b>, some <b>Mg²⁺</b>, lots of <b>proteins</b> (on average negatively charged) and <b>phosphate</b>. Extracellular: lots of <b>Na⁺</b>, <b>Cl⁻</b>, <b>HCO₃⁻</b>. These are averages – a snapshot.","w":["Swapped.","Na outside.","nM.","Not."],"k":"K⁺ inside; Na⁺/Cl⁻ outside.","rn":[["","Which anions are high inside the cell?","Which anions are high inside the cell?","Proteins; Phosphate (organic anions)"]]},{"id":19022,"t":"Fluid and Solutes of the Body","f":"pi20","s":"Why is a rise in cytosolic Ca²⁺ able to drive “dramatic changes” in a cell?","o":["Resting cytosolic Ca²⁺ is very low (100–200 nM), and many proteins respond","Resting cytosolic Ca²⁺ is very high (100–200 mM), and many proteins respond","Resting cytosolic Ca²⁺ is very low, and it inhibits most enzymes","Ca²⁺ is a non-ionic solute that crosses freely","Ca²⁺ affects only bone"],"e":"Ca²⁺ normally present at <b>very low levels in the cytosol (100–200 nM)</b>; many enzymes/proteins are activated by increased intracellular Ca²⁺ – e.g. <b>muscle contraction</b>, <b>exocytosis</b> of neurotransmitter. Ca²⁺ changes are <b>sharp pulses</b> because activating all this at once is <b>toxic</b>. The process figure is not for memorising.","w":["nM.","Activates.","Ion.","Not."],"k":"Low resting Ca²⁺; spikes activate.","rn":[["","Normal cytosolic calcium concentration?","Normal cytosolic calcium concentration?","100 to 200 nM"]]},{"id":19023,"t":"Diffusion and Osmosis","f":"pi21","s":"Which set lists the factors affecting the rate of diffusion?","o":["Concentration, temperature, viscosity and mass","Concentration, temperature, ATP and pH","Concentration, membrane proteins and ATP","Temperature only","Pressure, volume and colour"],"e":"Diffusion: movement of molecules <b>down their concentration gradient</b> (high → low); they never move the opposite way without help. Rate factors: <b>concentration</b> (steeper gradient), <b>temperature</b> (kinetic energy), <b>viscosity</b> (e.g. glycerol slows it), <b>mass</b> (½mv²).","w":["No ATP.","Simple.","More.","Not."],"k":"Diffusion: concentration, temperature, viscosity, mass.","rn":[["","What are the 4 factors affecting the rate of diffusion?","What are the 4 factors affecting the rate of diffusion?","Concentration; Viscosity; Temperature; Mass; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":19024,"t":"Diffusion and Osmosis","s":"Ink spreads quickly in warm water but slowly in glycerol. Name the property of glycerol responsible.","e":"Diffusion rate depends on concentration gradient, temperature, viscosity and mass.","k":"Viscosity.","a":["viscosity","high viscosity"],"rn":[["","What are the 4 factors affecting the rate of diffusion?","What are the 4 factors affecting the rate of diffusion?","Concentration; Viscosity; Temperature; Mass; MNEMONIC"],["","How does viscosity affect diffusion?","How does viscosity affect diffusion?","More viscous, slower"]]},{"id":19025,"t":"Diffusion and Osmosis","f":"pi22","s":"Water and NaCl solution are separated by a membrane permeable to water only. In which direction does net water movement occur, and why?","o":["Towards the NaCl side, where water concentration is lower","Towards the water side, where water concentration is lower","Towards the NaCl side, as salt pulls water by charge","No net movement, as the membrane blocks salt","Salt moves into the water side instead"],"e":"<b>Osmosis</b>: net diffusion of <b>water</b> down its concentration gradient. Na⁺ and Cl⁻ <b>displace water</b>, so water concentration is <b>less</b> on the NaCl side; net diffusion of water is <b>water → NaCl</b>. “It's just the net diffusion of water. It's really that unexciting.”","w":["Lower on NaCl.","Concentration.","Net movement.","Water only."],"k":"Water moves to lower water concentration.","rn":[["","Why is water concentration lower in NaCl solution?","Why is water concentration lower in NaCl solution?","Na+ and Cl- displace water"]]},{"id":19026,"t":"Diffusion and Osmosis","f":"pi23","s":"What is osmotic pressure and its principal determinant?","o":["Pressure needed to halt net water diffusion; set mainly by concentration","Pressure needed to halt net water diffusion; set mainly by temperature","Pressure of blood in the arteries","Pressure of air in the lungs","Force released by ATP hydrolysis"],"e":"<b>Osmotic pressure</b>: the pressure required to halt osmosis – the “pushback pressure”. Osmosis continues until the pressure difference between A and B halts it. Principal factor: <b>concentration</b>. <b>Osmolality</b>: osmolar concentration in osmoles per litre. At equilibrium there is still local exchange but no net movement.","w":["Concentration.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Osmotic pressure ∝ concentration.","rn":[["","Osmotic pressure","Osmotic pressure","Pressure to halt; Net water diffusion"]]},{"id":19027,"t":"Diffusion and Osmosis","f":"pi24","s":"Red blood cells are placed in a 200 mOsm/L solution. What happens?","o":["Water enters; the cell swells and may lyse","Water leaves; the cell shrinks (crenation)","No net water movement; no change","Salt enters; the cell shrinks","Water enters; the cell divides"],"e":"<b>Isotonic</b> (280 mOsm/L): no difference in [H₂O]; no change. <b>Hypotonic</b> (200): [H₂O] outside MORE than inside → water in → swells → <b>lysis</b>. <b>Hypertonic</b> (360): [H₂O] outside LESS → water out → shrinks → <b>crenation</b>.","w":["Hypertonic.","Isotonic.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Hypotonic → swell/lysis.","rn":[["","Hypotonic solution","Hypotonic solution","[H2O] outside MORE; Water enters, cell swells"]]},{"id":19028,"t":"Diffusion and Osmosis","s":"A red cell placed in 360 mOsm/L saline shrivels as water leaves. Name this appearance.","e":"Crenation: in a hypertonic solution water leaves and the cell shrivels; in a hypotonic one it swells and may lyse.","k":"Crenation.","a":["crenation","crenated","crenate"],"rn":[["","Crenation","Crenation","Cell shrinks in hypertonic solution"]]},{"id":19029,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Name the type of membrane protein that forms a pore through the bilayer.","e":"Channels form pores (e.g. aquaporins for water); carriers bind and change conformation.","k":"Channel.","a":["channel","membrane channel","ion channel","channel protein","channels"],"rn":[["","What is another name for a membrane channel?","What is another name for a membrane channel?","Protein channel"]]},{"id":19030,"t":"Membrane Transport","f":"pi28","s":"Which molecules can cross the lipid bilayer directly by simple diffusion?","o":["Oxygen and carbon dioxide","Glucose and amino acids","Na⁺ and K⁺","Water via aquaporins","Ca²⁺ and Cl⁻"],"e":"Passive transport is always related to diffusion down a concentration gradient. <b>O₂</b> and <b>CO₂</b> cross the bilayer directly – no help needed, but only move if more on one side.","w":["Carriers.","Channels.","Channel.","Charged."],"k":"O₂/CO₂ cross directly.","rn":[["","Which molecules cross the lipid bilayer by simple diffusion?","Which molecules cross the lipid bilayer by simple diffusion?","Oxygen; Carbon dioxide"]]},{"id":19031,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Name the specific channels that let water cross membranes rapidly.","e":"Aquaporins move ~1 billion water molecules per second passively; water also crosses the bilayer slowly.","k":"Aquaporins.","a":["aquaporins","aquaporin","aqp"],"rn":[["","Specific water channel example?","Specific water channel example?","Aquaporin"]]},{"id":19032,"t":"Membrane Transport","f":"pi30","s":"Glucose binds a carrier protein, which changes conformation and releases glucose inside. Where does the energy for this come from?","o":["The concentration gradient (facilitated diffusion)","ATP hydrolysis – it is primary active transport","The Na⁺ gradient – it is secondary active transport","The membrane potential alone","Light energy absorbed by the carrier"],"e":"<b>Carrier proteins change conformation after binding</b> the target and allow passage. Student question: the energy comes from glucose's interaction, which happens because of its <b>concentration gradient</b> – “really the energy is coming from the gradient”. Only ATP use = active. Why a carrier not a hole? Probably <b>regulation</b>.","w":["No ATP.","No Na⁺.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Facilitated carrier: gradient energy.","rn":[["","Where does energy for carrier shape change come from?","Where does energy for carrier shape change come from?","The concentration gradient"],["","What happens at very high glucose concentration?","What happens at very high glucose concentration?","Facilitated diffusion maxes out"],["","Facilitated diffusion","Facilitated diffusion","Passive transport; Helped by channel or carrier; Still down the gradient"]]},{"id":19033,"t":"Membrane Transport","f":"pi31","s":"As glucose concentration rises, glucose uptake via a carrier rises then plateaus, unlike simple diffusion which keeps rising linearly. Why?","o":["Carriers become saturated, reaching Vmax","ATP runs out at high glucose","Glucose becomes toxic at high levels","The membrane ruptures at high glucose","Simple diffusion also plateaus"],"e":"At high concentration the rate of facilitated diffusion <b>reaches a maximum</b> as the carrier becomes <b>saturated</b> – <b>Vmax</b>. Other molecules “have to wait their turn”. Simple diffusion: straight line. (Ignore D57A – mutation data.)","w":["No ATP.","Not.","Not.","Linear."],"k":"Carrier saturation = Vmax.","rn":[["","Vmax","Vmax","Maximum rate; Carrier saturated with target"],["","Why does facilitated diffusion plateau?","Why does facilitated diffusion plateau?","Carrier proteins become saturated"]]},{"id":19034,"t":"Membrane Transport","f":"pi32","s":"Glucose is high inside a cell and low outside, yet the cell keeps importing it with Na⁺. Which mechanism is this?","o":["Secondary active symport: Na⁺ down its gradient drives glucose in","Secondary active antiport: Na⁺ down its gradient drives glucose out","Primary active transport using ATP to import glucose","Facilitated diffusion of glucose down its gradient","Simple diffusion of glucose"],"e":"<b>Symporter</b>: outside high Na⁺, low glucose; inside low Na⁺, high glucose. <b>Na⁺ moves down</b> its gradient, allowing <b>glucose against</b> its gradient, same direction. Na⁺ passage causes a <b>conformational change</b> making the outer end “super attractive” to glucose; inner end less attractive, so glucose is released. “Cheap and elegant.” Secondary = “steal someone else's gradient”.","w":["Same direction.","Not directly.","Against.","Protein."],"k":"Na⁺-glucose symporter = 2° active.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of transporter is this? How does it work?","What type of transporter is this? How does it work?","Symporter (secondary active); Na+ down its gradient; Glucose against, same direction"]]},{"id":19035,"t":"Membrane Transport","f":"pi33","s":"In an Na⁺/H⁺ antiporter, which movements occur?","o":["Na⁺ in down its gradient; H⁺ out against its gradient","Na⁺ out against its gradient; H⁺ in down its gradient","Na⁺ and H⁺ both in","Na⁺ and H⁺ both out using ATP","Neither ion moves"],"e":"<b>Antiporter</b>: opposite directions using one component's gradient. Outside high Na⁺ and high H⁺; inside low both. <b>Na⁺ moves down</b> into the cell, allowing <b>H⁺ out against</b> its gradient.","w":["Reversed.","Opposite.","Not ATP.","Not."],"k":"Antiport: Na⁺ in, H⁺ out.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of transporter is this? How does it work?","What type of transporter is this? How does it work?","Symporter (secondary active); Na+ down its gradient; Glucose against, same direction"],["","Na+/H+ antiporter: which way does H+ move?","Na+/H+ antiporter: which way does H+ move?","Against its gradient; Out of the cell"],["","Na+/H+ antiporter: which way does Na+ move?","Na+/H+ antiporter: which way does Na+ move?","Down its gradient; Into the cell"]]},{"id":19036,"t":"Membrane Transport","f":"pi34","s":"Which describes the Na⁺-K⁺ ATP pump cycle?","o":["3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in, using ATP","2 Na⁺ out, 3 K⁺ in, using ATP","3 Na⁺ in, 2 K⁺ out, using ATP","3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in, using the Na⁺ gradient","3 K⁺ out, 2 Na⁺ in, using ATP"],"e":"<b>Primary active transport</b> uses <b>ATP</b> directly. 3 Na⁺ + ATP bind intracellularly; ATP hydrolysed; ADP released; pump changes shape and 3 Na⁺ go out; 2 K⁺ bind outside and go in; Pi released; resumes start. Both ions move <b>against</b> their gradients. Memory aid: “3 out, 2 in”.","w":["3 and 2.","Direction.","ATP.","Wrong."],"k":"Na-K pump: 3 Na out, 2 K in, ATP."},{"id":19037,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Why must the Na⁺-K⁺ pump exist if secondary transporters use the Na⁺ gradient?","o":["Using gradients runs them down; ATP restores them","Using gradients builds them up, so the pump removes the excess ions","The pump makes glucose for the symporters","The pump moves water to maintain volume","The pump makes HCl in all cells"],"e":"If we keep using Na⁺/K⁺ gradients to drive transport we mess up ion positions, so <b>primary active transport</b> re-establishes balance by hydrolysing ATP. The gradient powers secondary transport, the stomach example and neural transmission/action potentials.","w":["Runs down.","Not.","Not.","Stomach."],"k":"Pump restores gradients.","rn":[["","Why is the Na+/K+ pump needed?","Why is the Na+/K+ pump needed?","Re-establish Na+/K+ balance; Secondary transport runs down gradients"]]},{"id":19038,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Name the transport type that moves a substance against its gradient by borrowing another ion's gradient, without hydrolysing ATP itself.","e":"Secondary active transport (symport/antiport) uses gradients made by primary active transport (e.g. Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase).","k":"Secondary active transport.","a":["secondary active transport","secondary active","cotransport","co-transport"],"rn":[["","What are the 2 forms of active transport?","What are the 2 forms of active transport?","Primary (ATP); Secondary (gradient of another ion)"],["","Primary vs secondary active transport: energy source","Primary vs secondary active transport: energy source","Primary: ATP; Secondary: another ion's gradient"],["","Symporters and antiporters: transport type?","Symporters and antiporters: transport type?","Secondary active transport"]]},{"id":19039,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Which pair of transport types are classed as passive?","o":["Simple and facilitated diffusion","Primary and secondary active transport","Simple diffusion and secondary active transport","Facilitated diffusion and primary active transport","Osmosis and the Na-K pump"],"e":"Simple and facilitated diffusion = <b>passive</b> (with gradient, no ATP). Carriers tend to be specific; channels can be less specific, but many are very specific.","w":["Active.","Secondary is active.","Primary is active.","Pump is active."],"k":"Passive: simple + facilitated.","rn":[["","Which 2 transport types are passive?","Which 2 transport types are passive?","Simple diffusion; Facilitated diffusion"]]},{"id":19040,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"A ward patient develops cardiac arrhythmias and muscle cramps. Name the most likely electrolyte disturbance.","e":"Hyperkalaemia: arrhythmias and cramps; hypokalaemia: fatigue and twitching.","k":"Hyperkalaemia.","a":["hyperkalaemia","hyperkalemia","high potassium"],"rn":[["","Hyperkalaemia: 2 signs?","Hyperkalaemia: 2 signs?","Muscle cramps; Cardiac arrhythmias"]]},{"id":19041,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"A patient presents with tingling and numbness. Name the listed electrolyte disturbance.","e":"Hypocalcaemia: tingling and numbness; hypercalcaemia: nausea and confusion.","k":"Hypocalcaemia.","a":["hypocalcaemia","hypocalcemia","low calcium"],"rn":[["","Hypocalcaemia: 2 signs?","Hypocalcaemia: 2 signs?","Neurological issues; Tingling/numbness"]]},{"id":19042,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"What does the term “hyponatraemia” mean?","o":["Too little sodium in the blood","Too much sodium in the blood","Too little potassium in the blood","Too little calcium in the blood","Too much potassium in the blood"],"e":"Roots: <b>kal</b> = potassium (kalium), <b>natr</b> = sodium (natrium), <b>calc</b> = calcium; hyper = high, hypo = low; -aemia = in the blood. Hyponatraemia: neurological issues, headache, confusion.","w":["Hyper.","Kal.","Calc.","Hyperkal."],"k":"Hyponatraemia = low Na⁺.","rn":[["","Hyponatraemia","Hyponatraemia","Too little sodium"]]},{"id":19044,"t":"Fluid and Solutes of the Body","f":"pi39","s":"A solution has [H⁺] of 0.001 M. What is its pH, and how does it compare with a 0.1 M solution?","o":["pH 3; 100-fold less H⁺ than pH 1","pH 3; 2-fold less H⁺ than pH 1","pH 3; 100-fold more H⁺ than pH 1","pH 1; 100-fold more H⁺ than pH 3","pH 0.001; 10-fold less H⁺"],"e":"<b>pH = −log[H⁺]</b>. 0.1 M → pH 1; 0.001 M → pH 3. Each pH unit is a <b>10-fold</b> change, so 100-fold less H⁺. Lower pH = more acidic. Because it's a log scale, small pH changes mean big concentration changes.","w":["Log scale.","Less.","Not.","Not."],"k":"pH log scale.","rn":[["","Normal cytosolic calcium concentration?","Normal cytosolic calcium concentration?","100 to 200 nM"]]},{"id":19045,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"Which statement about physiological pH is correct?","o":["ECF 7.40, slightly less acidic than cytosol; regulated by lungs and kidneys","ECF 7.40, slightly more acidic than cytosol; regulated by lungs and kidneys","ECF 7.0, slightly less acidic than cytosol; regulated by the skin","Plasma pH is unregulated","ECF 1.0, strongly acidic"],"e":"Intra- and extracellular pH are tightly regulated: <b>ECF pH 7.40</b>, somewhat <b>less acidic than cytosol (7–7.4)</b>. Plasma pH regulated via <b>lungs and kidneys</b>. Formally pH = −log[H₃O⁺] (hydronium), but −log[H⁺] is used for convenience.","w":["Less acidic.","Not.","Regulated.","Not."],"k":"ECF 7.40; cytosol 7–7.4.","rn":[["","Plasma pH is regulated via which 2 organs?","Plasma pH is regulated via which 2 organs?","Lungs; Kidneys"]]},{"id":19046,"t":"pH and the Stomach","f":"pi40","s":"What are the functions of HCl secreted by parietal cells?","o":["Digestion, killing pathogens, activating proteases","Digesting food and producing bicarbonate for the blood","Absorbing glucose from the stomach","Contracting stomach smooth muscle","Producing protective mucus"],"e":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells release <b>HCl (~160 mM)</b>: helps <b>digest food</b>, <b>protects</b> from pathogens; the <b>low pH stimulates proteolytic enzymes</b> (“molecular scissors”). Mucous neck cells sit near the top of the gland.","w":["Bicarbonate is a by-product to plasma.","Not.","Not.","Neck cells."],"k":"HCl roles.","rn":[["","How does HCl protect against pathogens?","How does HCl protect against pathogens?","High HCl kills some bacteria"]]},{"id":19047,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"Name the enzyme in the parietal cell that converts CO₂ and water into carbonic acid.","e":"Carbonic anhydrase: CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ HCO₃⁻ + H⁺; the direction depends on tissue (bicarbonate made in tissues, CO₂ released in lungs).","k":"Carbonic anhydrase.","a":["carbonic anhydrase"],"rn":[["","Carbonic anhydrase","Carbonic anhydrase","Enzyme: H2O + CO2; To carbonic acid; Then bicarbonate"],["","Carbonic anhydrase in other tissues","Carbonic anhydrase in other tissues","H2O + CO2 to H2CO3; Then HCO3- + H+"],["","What decides the direction of the carbonic anhydrase reaction?","What decides the direction of the carbonic anhydrase reaction?","The environment / pH"]]},{"id":19048,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"Name the primary active pump that moves H⁺ from the parietal cell into the gastric lumen.","e":"The H⁺/K⁺ ATPase (proton pump) on the luminal side uses ATP; omeprazole inhibits it.","k":"H⁺/K⁺ ATPase.","a":["h+/k+ atpase","h k atpase","proton pump","h+-k+ atpase","hydrogen potassium atpase","h+ k+ atpase","h/k atpase"],"rn":[["","What transports H+ into the gland lumen?","What transports H+ into the gland lumen?","H+/K+ ATPase pump; Gastric proton pump"],["","How does omeprazole inhibit the proton pump?","How does omeprazole inhibit the proton pump?","Disulphide bond; To H+/K+ ATPase residues"]]},{"id":19049,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"Bicarbonate builds up inside the parietal cell. How does the cell remove it, and what does it gain?","o":["AE2 exports HCO₃⁻ to plasma as Cl⁻ enters","AE2 exports HCO₃⁻ to the lumen as Cl⁻ enters","HCO₃⁻ diffuses freely into the lumen","HCO₃⁻ is pumped into the lumen using ATP","HCO₃⁻ stays inside the cell"],"e":"HCO₃⁻ is transported to the <b>plasma</b> via the <b>Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ anion exchanger 2 (AE2)</b> – an antiporter coupling the chloride gradient (more Cl⁻ outside) to export bicarbonate. Cl⁻ then goes into the lumen via <b>CIC-2</b>, so HCl rises to ~160 mM.","w":["Plasma.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"AE2: HCO₃⁻ out, Cl⁻ in."},{"id":19050,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"Why does the parietal cell recycle K⁺ via KCNJ15?","o":["Clean-up: stops K⁺ leaking and upsetting the gradient","To combine K⁺ with Cl⁻ in the lumen to make KCl acid","To produce bicarbonate for the plasma","To stop CO₂ entering the cell","To generate ATP for the pump"],"e":"Why recycle K⁺? “Molecular clean-up”: otherwise potassium slowly leaks the wrong way and the overall gradient is disturbed. The cell is a good “molecular accountant”.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"K⁺ recycling preserves gradients.","rn":[["","Why recycle K+?","Why recycle K+?","Avoid leaking K+ wrongly; Keep ion gradients"]]},{"id":19051,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Which combination of transport types does the parietal cell use to make HCl?","o":["Simple diffusion, primary active, secondary antiport and channels","Simple diffusion, primary active and facilitated glucose carriers","Primary active transport only","Simple diffusion only","Osmosis and aquaporins only"],"e":"One cell shows <b>simple diffusion</b>, <b>primary</b> and <b>secondary active transport</b> working together, plus K⁺ and Cl⁻ export channels.","w":["Not glucose.","More.","More.","Not."],"k":"Parietal cell integrates transport types.","rn":[["","Example of primary and secondary active transport","Example of primary and secondary active transport","Primary: Na+/K+ pump; Secondary: Na+/glucose symporter"],["","Primary vs secondary active transport: energy source","Primary vs secondary active transport: energy source","Primary: ATP; Secondary: another ion's gradient"],["","What are the 2 forms of active transport?","What are the 2 forms of active transport?","Primary (ATP); Secondary (gradient of another ion)"]]},{"id":19052,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"A patient with heartburn is given omeprazole. Name its drug class.","e":"Omeprazole is a PPI binding the H⁺/K⁺ ATPase, so fewer protons are pumped.","k":"PPI.","a":["proton pump inhibitor","ppi","proton pump inhibitors","ppis"],"rn":[["","Omeprazole acts as what?","Omeprazole acts as what?","Proton pump inhibitor (PPI)"]]},{"id":19053,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"Why are oxonol dyes more harmful than inhibiting the proton pump alone?","o":["They inhibit the Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ antiporter, upsetting bicarbonate","They inhibit only the H⁺/K⁺ pump, like a stronger omeprazole","They activate carbonic anhydrase","They block the Na-K pump only","They are harmless dyes"],"e":"<b>Oxonol dyes</b> inhibit the <b>chloride–bicarbonate antiporter</b> (and other transporters); this can be <b>dangerous</b> as it disturbs the <b>bicarbonate cycle</b>.","w":["Antiporter.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Oxonol dyes inhibit AE2."},{"id":19054,"t":"pH and the Stomach","f":"pi45","s":"In which direction does carbonic anhydrase drive the reaction in the lungs, and why does this make sense?","o":["HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂, producing CO₂ to breathe out","H₂O + CO₂ → HCO₃⁻ + H⁺, producing CO₂ to breathe out","HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ → H₂O + O₂, producing O₂","It does not act in the lungs","It produces HCl in the lungs"],"e":"Carbonic anhydrase: H₂O + CO₂ ⇌ HCO₃⁻ + H⁺; direction <b>driven by pH</b> and environment. <b>Lungs</b>: → <b>H₂O + CO₂</b> (breathed out). <b>Other tissues</b>: → HCO₃⁻ + H⁺. Notes: oxygenated haemoglobin in the lungs becomes more acidic, reducing CO₂ affinity and releasing H⁺, which combines with HCO₃⁻.","w":["Wrong direction.","Not.","It does.","Not."],"k":"Lungs: CA makes CO₂."},{"id":19056,"t":"Electrochemical Gradients","f":"pi48","s":"Which statement about biological voltage is correct?","o":["A potential difference from membrane properties and ions","A potential difference produced by ATP alone","A potential difference from ions crossing the bilayer freely","A positive interior relative to the outside","Unrelated to ion concentrations"],"e":"<b>Voltage</b>: potential difference between two points in an electrical field. Biologically: consequence of <b>membrane properties</b> and <b>ion concentrations</b>; membranes are <b>not normally permeable</b> to charged ions; ions are moved by active and passive processes. <b>K⁺</b> is the majority cation inside, <b>Na⁺</b> outside; Cl⁻ also important. Outside generally <b>more positive</b>, inside more negative (Vm).","w":["Not.","Not permeable.","Negative.","Related."],"k":"Voltage from ion distribution.","rn":[["","Summary: the distribution of ions is a consequence of what?","Summary: the distribution of ions is a consequence of what?","Membrane properties; Electrical and chemical forces"],["","Voltage","Voltage","Potential difference; Between two field points"],["","Vm","Vm","Membrane voltage (membrane potential)"]]},{"id":19057,"t":"Electrochemical Gradients","f":"pi49","s":"With a membrane potential (outside +, inside −), why does a positive ion have a larger inward driving force than a negative ion with the same concentration gradient?","o":["For the cation, chemical and electrical forces both push in","For the anion, chemical and electrical forces both push in","Cations use ATP to enter","Anions carry no charge","Voltage has no effect on ions"],"e":"Electrochemical gradient = <b>chemical</b> (concentration) + <b>electrical</b> (charge). Positive ion: big chemical gradient in and attracted by negative inside → big arrow. Negative ion: chemical gradient in, but <b>repelled</b> by negative inside → thin arrow. “Super important” for neuron physiology next year – “you need to learn it”.","w":["Opposed.","Not.","Charged.","It does."],"k":"Electrochemical = chemical + electrical.","rn":[["","Summary: the distribution of ions is a consequence of what?","Summary: the distribution of ions is a consequence of what?","Membrane properties; Electrical and chemical forces"]]},{"id":19058,"t":"Electrochemical Gradients","s":"Which is <b>not</b> one of the rules of ionic balance?","o":["Ions never need to be replaced once they leave","Positive and negative ions must nearly balance","An ion leaving must soon be replaced by another of that type coming in","Energy is always being used to re-establish gradients","(All of the others are rules)"],"e":"Rules: (1) positive and negative ions must <b>nearly balance</b>; (2) any ion that <b>leaves must be replaced soon</b> by another of that type coming in; (3) <b>energy is always being used</b> to re-establish gradients. The cell is a good “accountant”.","w":["Rule.","Rule.","Rule.","Option 1 is not a rule."],"k":"Three rules of ionic balance."},{"id":19059,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"Which of these cells is non-excitable?","o":["A gastric parietal cell","A neuron","A skeletal muscle cell","A smooth muscle cell","A cardiac myocyte"],"e":"<b>Excitable</b> cells produce or respond to electrical signals and propagate action potentials: <b>neurons, skeletal muscle, smooth muscle, cardiac myocytes</b>. <b>Non-excitable</b>: everything else – e.g. the parietal cell (the lecture's non-excitable example of ionic activity).","w":["Excitable.","Excitable.","Excitable.","Excitable."],"k":"Parietal cell = non-excitable.","rn":[["","Is a parietal cell excitable?","Is a parietal cell excitable?","No, non-excitable"],["","Parietal cell is an example of what type of cell?","Parietal cell is an example of what type of cell?","Non-excitable"]]},{"id":19060,"t":"Electrochemical Gradients","s":"What defines an excitable cell?","o":["It produces or responds to electrical signals","It produces electrical signals but cannot respond to them","It responds to chemical signals only","It secretes acid","It lacks a membrane potential"],"e":"Excitable cells can <b>produce OR respond to electrical signals</b> and <b>propagate action potentials</b>. More with the neuromuscular junction.","w":["Or responds.","Electrical.","Not.","Has one."],"k":"Excitable definition.","rn":[["","Excitable cells","Excitable cells","Make/respond to electrical signals; Propagate action potentials"]]},{"id":19061,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"A drug blocks the Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase. Over time, what would happen to Na⁺-glucose symport into cells?","o":["It would fall, as the Na⁺ gradient would run down","It would rise, as more Na⁺ would stay outside","It would be unchanged, as symporters use ATP directly","It would reverse, exporting glucose with ATP","Glucose would enter by osmosis instead"],"e":"Secondary active transport “steals” the Na⁺ gradient; the <b>Na-K pump</b> (primary, ATP) maintains it. Block the pump and the gradient dissipates, so the symporter loses its driving force.","w":["Gradient lost.","No ATP.","Not.","Not."],"k":"2° transport depends on the pump.","rn":[["","Why is the Na+/K+ pump needed?","Why is the Na+/K+ pump needed?","Re-establish Na+/K+ balance; Secondary transport runs down gradients"],["","Image card: What type of transporter is this? How does it work?","What type of transporter is this? How does it work?","Symporter (secondary active); Na+ down its gradient; Glucose against, same direction"],["","Passive Transport","Passive Transport","Movement down the concentration gradient; No help, no energy needed"]]},{"id":19062,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Which transport process in the parietal cell would continue unchanged if ATP ran out, at least initially?","o":["CO₂ entry by simple diffusion","H⁺/K⁺ ATPase proton pumping","Na⁺-K⁺ pump activity","Maintenance of all ion gradients indefinitely","HCl secretion at 160 mM"],"e":"CO₂ enters by <b>simple diffusion</b> (passive) and needs no ATP. The H⁺/K⁺ ATPase (primary active) needs ATP; gradients would run down.","w":["Needs ATP.","Needs ATP.","Energy needed.","Needs pumping."],"k":"Simple diffusion needs no ATP.","rn":[["","Which transport type needs no membrane protein?","Which transport type needs no membrane protein?","Simple diffusion"],["","How does CO2 enter the parietal cell?","How does CO2 enter the parietal cell?","Passive transport (simple diffusion)"],["","What is needed for simple diffusion into a cell?","What is needed for simple diffusion into a cell?","More outside than inside"]]},{"id":19063,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"In which situation does a variable get “amplified but not controlled”?","o":["Positive feedback","Negative feedback","Isotonic conditions","Simple diffusion equilibrium","Baroreflex at rest"],"e":"<b>Positive feedback</b>: self-perpetuating cycle where the variable is amplified but not controlled → instability.","w":["Controlled.","Not.","Not.","Negative."],"k":"Amplified = positive feedback.","rn":[["","Positive feedback","Positive feedback","Self-perpetuating cycle; Amplified, not controlled; Instability, lost equilibrium"]]},{"id":19064,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"A patient's blood pressure falls after haemorrhage. Which homeostatic response would first try to correct it?","o":["Relaxed baroreceptors → more vasomotor activity → vasoconstriction, faster heart","Stretched baroreceptors → less vasomotor activity → vasodilation, slower heart","Relaxed baroreceptors → less vasomotor activity → vasodilation","Sweating to restore volume","Oxytocin release to constrict vessels"],"e":"<b>Low BP</b>: relaxed baroreceptors → <b>increased activity</b> of the vasomotor centre → <b>vasoconstriction</b> and <b>increased heart rate</b>. At 1 L loss this suffices.","w":["High BP.","More.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Low BP → vasoconstriction + ↑HR.","rn":[["","Low BP: what happens to vasomotor centre activity?","Low BP: what happens to vasomotor centre activity?","Increased activity"],["","Low BP: what happens to the baroreceptors?","Low BP: what happens to the baroreceptors?","Relaxed"],["","What are the only 2 ways to increase blood pressure?","What are the only 2 ways to increase blood pressure?","Vasoconstriction; Make the heart pump faster"]]},{"id":19065,"t":"Diffusion and Osmosis","s":"IV fluid given to a patient is much more dilute than plasma. What risk does this pose to red cells, using the tonicity principles?","o":["Water enters; red cells swell and may lyse","Water leaves red cells (hypertonic), so they crenate","No net water movement (isotonic)","Red cells pump water out using ATP","Plasma becomes hypertonic"],"e":"A dilute (hypotonic) solution has more water outside than inside → water enters → cells swell → <b>lysis</b>.","w":["Dilute = hypotonic.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Hypotonic → lysis.","rn":[["","Hypotonic solution","Hypotonic solution","[H2O] outside MORE; Water enters, cell swells"]]},{"id":19066,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Which statement explains why charged ions like Na⁺ need channels but O₂ does not?","o":["Charged ions cannot cross the hydrophobic bilayer; O₂ can","Charged ions are too large to cross the bilayer; O₂ is small","O₂ needs ATP to cross; Na⁺ does not","Na⁺ is non-ionic and crosses freely","O₂ needs aquaporins"],"e":"If a molecule is <b>highly charged</b>, it cannot cross the lipid bilayer, so we “make a hole” (channel). O₂ and CO₂ cross directly by simple diffusion.","w":["Charge, not size.","Not.","Charged.","Not."],"k":"Charge needs channels.","rn":[["","Can highly charged molecules cross the bilayer?","Can highly charged molecules cross the bilayer?","No, need a hole (channel)"]]},{"id":19067,"t":"Diffusion and Osmosis","s":"Which pair of definitions is correct?","o":["Osmosis: net water diffusion; osmolality: osmoles per litre","Osmosis: net salt diffusion; osmolality: osmoles per litre","Osmosis: active water transport; osmolality: pressure","Osmosis: net water diffusion; osmolality: pressure to halt it","Osmosis: ATP use; osmolality: pH"],"e":"<b>Osmosis</b>: net diffusion of water. <b>Osmolality</b>: osmolar concentration in osmoles per litre of solution.","w":["Water.","Passive.","Osmotic pressure.","Not."],"k":"Osmosis and osmolality.","rn":[["","Osmolality","Osmolality","Osmolar concentration; Osmoles per litre"],["","Osmosis","Osmosis","Net water diffusion; Down its gradient"]]},{"id":19068,"t":"Fluid and Solutes of the Body","s":"A cell experiences a sudden prolonged high cytosolic Ca²⁺ level. Why might this be harmful?","o":["Activating many processes at once is toxic","Ca²⁺ blocks all enzymes when present at high levels","Ca²⁺ cannot enter cells","Ca²⁺ lowers osmotic pressure","Ca²⁺ is inert"],"e":"Ca²⁺ change is a <b>sharp pulse</b>, because activating all this at once tends to be <b>toxic to the cell</b>.","w":["Activates.","It does.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Prolonged Ca²⁺ is toxic.","rn":[["","Why is calcium released as a pulse?","Why is calcium released as a pulse?","Activating everything is toxic"]]},{"id":19069,"t":"Electrochemical Gradients","s":"Which statement about the direction of the electrochemical gradient for Na⁺ at rest is correct?","o":["Na⁺ wants in: high outside and drawn to the negative inside","Na⁺ wants out: high inside and repelled by the negative inside","Na⁺ wants in: high outside but repelled by the negative inside","Na⁺ has no net gradient at rest","Na⁺ is higher inside the cell"],"e":"Na⁺ is high outside, low inside; the inside is negative – both components drive Na⁺ <b>in</b> (“sodium wants to get in, potassium wants to get out”).","w":["Not.","Attracted.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Na⁺ driven inwards.","rn":[["","Anion outside, negative inside: electrochemical gradient?","Anion outside, negative inside: electrochemical gradient?","Reduced; Repelled by negative inside"],["","Na+/H+ antiporter: concentrations","Na+/H+ antiporter: concentrations","High Na+ outside, low inside; High H+ outside, low inside"],["","Na+/K+ pump: K+ concentrations","Na+/K+ pump: K+ concentrations","Low outside; High inside"]]},{"id":19070,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Name the transporter type that moves two species in the same direction.","e":"Symporter: same direction (e.g. Na⁺-glucose); antiporter: opposite directions (e.g. Na⁺/H⁺).","k":"Symporter.","a":["symporter","symport","cotransporter"],"rn":[["","Symporter vs antiporter: direction","Symporter vs antiporter: direction","Symporter: same direction; Antiporter: opposite directions"]]},{"id":19071,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Why is the Na⁺-K⁺ pump unable to use concentration gradients to move ions?","o":["It moves both ions against their gradients, so needs ATP","It moves both ions with their gradients, so needs no ATP","It moves Na⁺ with and K⁺ against its gradient","It is a channel, not a pump","It uses light energy"],"e":"It cannot use gradients because it moves <b>both ions against their gradients</b> – it needs ATP.","w":["Against.","Both against.","Pump.","Not."],"k":"Pump: both against gradients.","rn":[["","Why can't the Na+/K+ pump use a gradient?","Why can't the Na+/K+ pump use a gradient?","Both ions move against gradients"],["","Active Transport","Active Transport","Movement against the concentration gradient; Needs energy"]]},{"id":19072,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Which explanation of “secondary” in secondary active transport is correct?","o":["It borrows a gradient made by primary transport","It is less important than primary transport in cells","It happens after diffusion in time","It uses two ATP molecules","It moves water"],"e":"“Secondary = steal someone else's concentration gradient. Primary = use ATP.”","w":["Not.","Not.","No ATP.","Not."],"k":"Secondary = borrowed gradient.","rn":[["","Primary vs secondary active transport: energy source","Primary vs secondary active transport: energy source","Primary: ATP; Secondary: another ion's gradient"],["","What are the 2 forms of active transport?","What are the 2 forms of active transport?","Primary (ATP); Secondary (gradient of another ion)"],["","Simple memory for secondary active transport?","Simple memory for secondary active transport?","Steal someone else's gradient"]]},{"id":19073,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Which pair of examples matches simple diffusion and facilitated diffusion?","o":["CO₂ crossing the bilayer; glucose via a carrier","Glucose via a carrier; CO₂ crossing the bilayer","Na-K pump; Na-glucose symporter","H⁺/K⁺ ATPase; AE2 exchanger","Aquaporin; Na-K pump"],"e":"Simple diffusion: CO₂ (no protein). Facilitated: glucose carrier, aquaporin (protein, passive).","w":["Swapped.","Active.","Active.","Pump."],"k":"Examples per transport type.","rn":[["","Is facilitated diffusion via a carrier still passive?","Is facilitated diffusion via a carrier still passive?","Yes, no ATP"],["","Example molecule moved by a carrier protein?","Example molecule moved by a carrier protein?","Glucose"]]},{"id":19074,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"What does the reaction direction of carbonic anhydrase in tissues (CO₂ + H₂O → HCO₃⁻ + H⁺) achieve?","o":["CO₂ → bicarbonate and protons for acid–base balance","Releases CO₂ from bicarbonate so that it can be breathed out","Produces O₂ for tissues","Makes glucose from CO₂","Stops pH regulation"],"e":"In other tissues CA drives CO₂ + H₂O → HCO₃⁻ + H⁺; in lungs the reverse. The enzyme is involved in <b>pH acid/base homeostasis</b>.","w":["Lungs.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"CA in tissues: CO₂ → HCO₃⁻.","rn":[["","Carbonic anhydrase is involved in what homeostasis?","Carbonic anhydrase is involved in what homeostasis?","pH acid/base homeostasis"],["","Carbonic anhydrase","Carbonic anhydrase","Enzyme: H2O + CO2; To carbonic acid; Then bicarbonate"]]},{"id":19075,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"Name the stretch-sensitive receptors in vessel walls that detect blood pressure.","e":"Baroreceptors are mechanical stretch receptors (sensor); the medullary vasomotor centres are the controller.","k":"Baroreceptors.","a":["baroreceptors","baroreceptor","arterial baroreceptors"],"rn":[["","Baroreceptors are also what type of receptor?","Baroreceptors are also what type of receptor?","Mechanical receptors (stretch)"]]},{"id":19076,"t":"Diffusion and Osmosis","s":"Which statement about osmotic equilibrium in a U-tube is correct?","o":["Local exchange continues, with no net movement","All water movement stops completely","Solute crosses the membrane until concentrations equalise","Pressure is zero on both sides","The tube is sealed at both ends"],"e":"At that point there is still <b>local exchange</b> but no net motion. Both tops are open to atmosphere; not sealed.","w":["Local exchange.","Water only.","Not.","Open."],"k":"Equilibrium: no net movement.","rn":[["","Do water molecules still exchange at equilibrium?","Do water molecules still exchange at equilibrium?","Yes, local exchange; No net movement"]]},{"id":19078,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"What is the significance of the double arrow in H₂O + CO₂ ⇌ HCO₃⁻ + H⁺?","o":["Reversible; direction depends on tissue and pH","Two different enzymes drive each direction of the reaction","The reaction uses ATP in both directions","It is a positive feedback loop","The reaction never occurs"],"e":"The interesting thing is the <b>double arrow</b>: direction depends on tissue and environment, driven by pH.","w":["One.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Reversible CA reaction."},{"id":19079,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"Which are examples of homeostatically controlled variables?","o":["Body temperature, blood pressure and blood pH","Body temperature, blood pressure and blood group","Blood pH, height and weight","Hair colour and eye colour","Number of teeth"],"e":"Variables: <b>body temperature</b>, <b>blood pressure</b>, <b>blood pH</b>; if blood pH changes it alters blood proteins and brings about failure.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Homeostatic variables.","rn":[["","Give 3 examples of variables controlled by homeostasis.","Give 3 examples of variables controlled by homeostasis.","Body temperature; Blood pressure; Blood pH"]]},{"id":19080,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Which statement best describes why a Na⁺-glucose symporter releases glucose inside rather than letting it go back out?","o":["The inner end binds glucose poorly, releasing it","The inner end binds glucose tightly, so it stays bound to the carrier","ATP pumps glucose out at the inner end","Na⁺ binds glucose permanently","Osmosis draws glucose in"],"e":"The inner end is much <b>less attractive</b> to glucose, so it is released into the cell and cannot go back (and is soon used, e.g. oxidised). Na⁺ changes the protein, not binding glucose.","w":["Poorly.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Symporter affinity change.","rn":[["","Why can't glucose go back out of the symporter?","Why can't glucose go back out of the symporter?","Conformation stops it; Inner end unattractive"]]},{"id":19081,"t":"Fluid and Solutes of the Body","s":"Which statement about proteins inside the cell is correct?","o":["They are charged, on average negatively","They are charged, on average positively","They are uncharged","They are mainly extracellular","They are absent from the cytoplasm"],"e":"Proteins tend to be charged; <b>on average negative</b> (not always); mainly inside cells.","w":["Negative.","Charged.","Inside.","Present."],"k":"Intracellular proteins negative."},{"id":19082,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"Which of the following is the best example of negative feedback?","o":["Sweating when core temperature rises","Oxytocin release during labour","Heart failure after 2 L blood loss","A Ca²⁺ spike activating enzymes","HCl secretion by parietal cells"],"e":"Thermoregulation (sweating when too hot) and baroreceptors are negative feedback examples.","w":["Positive.","Positive.","Not feedback.","Not."],"k":"Sweating = negative feedback.","rn":[["","Which body temperature is monitored and controlled?","Which body temperature is monitored and controlled?","Core temperature; Not the fingers"],["","What monitors core temperature?","What monitors core temperature?","A neural mechanism"]]},{"id":19083,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"What is the approximate HCl concentration in the gastric lumen?","o":["About 160 mM","About 4 mM","About 1.8 mM","About 0.001 mM","About 1 M"],"e":"HCl concentration ≈ <b>160 mM</b>.","w":["K⁺.","Ca.","Not.","Not."],"k":"HCl ≈ 160 mM.","rn":[["","What is the HCl concentration in the stomach?","What is the HCl concentration in the stomach?","About 160 mM"]]},{"id":19086,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Which explanation of why facilitated diffusion curves are hyperbolic is correct?","o":["A finite number of carriers become saturated","An unlimited number of carriers keep increasing","ATP supply limits the curve","Water dilutes the solute","The membrane changes thickness"],"e":"All carriers become <b>saturated</b>; add enough glucose and they cannot go faster (Vmax).","w":["Finite.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Saturation → hyperbola.","rn":[["","Why does facilitated diffusion plateau?","Why does facilitated diffusion plateau?","Carrier proteins become saturated"]]},{"id":19087,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"A patient is breathing fast and restless; biochemistry shows abnormal sodium. Name the disturbance.","e":"Hypernatraemia is listed with fast breathing and restlessness.","k":"Hypernatraemia.","a":["hypernatraemia","hypernatremia","high sodium"],"rn":[["","Hypernatraemia: 2 signs?","Hypernatraemia: 2 signs?","Fast breathing; Restlessness"]]},{"id":19088,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Which imbalance is associated with fatigue and muscle twitching?","o":["Hypokalaemia","Hyperkalaemia","Hypercalcaemia","Hyponatraemia","Hypernatraemia"],"e":"<b>Hypokalaemia</b>: fatigue; muscle twitching.","w":["Cramps, arrhythmia.","Nausea, confusion.","Headache.","Restlessness."],"k":"Hypokalaemia symptoms.","rn":[["","Hypokalaemia: 2 signs?","Hypokalaemia: 2 signs?","Fatigue; Muscle twitching"]]},{"id":19089,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"A patient has nausea and confusion. Name the calcium disturbance listed with these symptoms.","e":"Hypercalcaemia causes nausea and confusion.","k":"Hypercalcaemia.","a":["hypercalcaemia","hypercalcemia","high calcium"],"rn":[["","Hypercalcaemia: 2 signs?","Hypercalcaemia: 2 signs?","Nausea; Confusion"]]},{"id":19090,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"Which statement about where the H⁺/K⁺ ATPase and AE2 are located in the parietal cell is correct?","o":["H⁺/K⁺ ATPase on the lumen side; AE2 on the plasma side","H⁺/K⁺ ATPase on the plasma side; AE2 on the lumen side","Both on the plasma side","Both on the lumen side","Both in the mitochondria"],"e":"H⁺/K⁺ ATPase, KCNJ15 and CIC-2: lumen side. AE2: extracellular (plasma) side.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Transporter locations.","rn":[["","What transports H+ into the gland lumen?","What transports H+ into the gland lumen?","H+/K+ ATPase pump; Gastric proton pump"]]},{"id":19091,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"Which drug would reduce acid most directly at the final step of proton secretion?","o":["Omeprazole (H⁺/K⁺ ATPase)","An oxonol dye, inhibiting AE2","A carbonic anhydrase activator","A Na-K pump activator","An aquaporin blocker"],"e":"Omeprazole inhibits the H⁺/K⁺ ATPase – the pump that moves H⁺ into the lumen.","w":["Not final step.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"PPI blocks final step.","rn":[["","How does omeprazole inhibit the proton pump?","How does omeprazole inhibit the proton pump?","Disulphide bond; To H+/K+ ATPase residues"]]},{"id":19092,"t":"Physiology and Pathophysiology","s":"Which statement about the levels at which physiology is studied is correct?","o":["Cell, organ and whole body","Molecular level only","Organ level only","Whole-body level only","Cellular level only"],"e":"Physiology can be investigated at <b>cellular</b>, <b>organ</b> and <b>whole-body system</b> levels.","w":["More.","More.","More.","More."],"k":"Physiology levels.","rn":[["","At what 3 levels can physiology be investigated?","At what 3 levels can physiology be investigated?","Cellular; Organ; Entire body as a system"],["","Physiology tends to look at what level?","Physiology tends to look at what level?","Whole body, whole tissue"]]},{"id":19093,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Which pairing of transport type and specificity is correct?","o":["Facilitated – usually specific; active – specific","Facilitated – non-specific; active – non-specific","Simple diffusion – specific; active – specific","Facilitated – usually specific; active – non-specific","All transport types are non-specific"],"e":"Specific: simple n/a; facilitated <b>usually</b>; 1° active yes; 2° active yes.","w":["Specific.","n/a.","Specific.","Not."],"k":"Specificity column.","rn":[["","Facilitated diffusion: specific?","Facilitated diffusion: specific?","Usually"]]},{"id":19094,"t":"Fluid and Solutes of the Body","s":"What is the result of activating many enzymes when intracellular Ca²⁺ increases, as in a neuron's synapse?","o":["Exocytosis of neurotransmitter","Osmosis of water","HCl secretion","Crenation","Positive feedback in labour"],"e":"Ca²⁺ examples: <b>muscle contraction</b> and <b>exocytosis</b> (neurotransmitter release across the synaptic cleft).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Ca²⁺ → exocytosis.","rn":[["","Calcium is needed for exocytosis of what?","Calcium is needed for exocytosis of what?","Neurotransmitters across the synaptic cleft"]]},{"id":19095,"t":"Diffusion and Osmosis","s":"Why does a red cell in a 280 mOsm/L solution show no change?","o":["Isotonic: no water gradient","Hypotonic: water enters, but slowly","Hypertonic: water leaves, but slowly","ATP prevents any change","Salt enters and balances water"],"e":"Isotonic: water concentration about the same inside and outside; no gradient; nothing happens.","w":["Not.","Isotonic.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Isotonic = no net movement.","rn":[["","Osmosis","Osmosis","Net water diffusion; Down its gradient"]]},{"id":19096,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"Which protons source is minor in the parietal cell?","o":["Dissociation of water","Carbonic acid dissociation","H⁺/K⁺ ATPase","Stomach mucus","Plasma bicarbonate"],"e":"Some protons may come from <b>dissociation of H₂O</b> – minor; the main source is H₂CO₃ dissociation.","w":["Main.","Pump.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Water dissociation minor.","rn":[["","Where does the proton for HCl come from?","Where does the proton for HCl come from?","Dissociation of H2CO3"]]},{"id":19097,"t":"Electrochemical Gradients","s":"Which ions are the majority cations inside and outside the cell respectively?","o":["K⁺ inside; Na⁺ outside","Na⁺ inside; K⁺ outside","Ca²⁺ inside; Mg²⁺ outside","H⁺ inside; Cl⁻ outside","Both K⁺"],"e":"K⁺ forms most cations inside; Na⁺ outside. Memory aid: “salty sea outside, potassium palace inside”.","w":["Reversed.","Not.","Cl⁻ is an anion.","Not."],"k":"K⁺ in, Na⁺ out.","rn":[["","Anion outside, negative inside: electrochemical gradient?","Anion outside, negative inside: electrochemical gradient?","Reduced; Repelled by negative inside"],["","What is needed for simple diffusion into a cell?","What is needed for simple diffusion into a cell?","More outside than inside"],["","Na+/H+ antiporter: concentrations","Na+/H+ antiporter: concentrations","High Na+ outside, low inside; High H+ outside, low inside"]]},{"id":19101,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"A student says water only crosses membranes via aquaporins. Which correction is accurate?","o":["It crosses the bilayer slowly; mostly via channels","Water never crosses the bilayer","Water crosses only through ATP-driven pumps","Water crosses only via symporters","Water crosses via saturable carriers"],"e":"Common misconception: water <b>can cross the membrane, but really slowly</b>; generally via channels.","w":["Slowly.","Passive.","Channels.","Channels."],"k":"Water crosses slowly without channels.","rn":[["","Does water mainly cross the bilayer directly?","Does water mainly cross the bilayer directly?","No, slowly; Mainly through protein channels"]]},{"id":19102,"t":"Diffusion and Osmosis","s":"Which of the following correctly matches mass with diffusion rate?","o":["Mass affects kinetic energy; bigger may be slower","Mass has no effect on diffusion","Heavier molecules always diffuse faster","Mass only affects osmosis","Mass determines pH"],"e":"<b>Mass</b> links to kinetic energy ½mv²; bigger molecules may also have a bigger surface area.","w":["It does.","Slower.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Mass affects diffusion.","rn":[["","How does temperature affect diffusion?","How does temperature affect diffusion?","Raises kinetic energy of molecules"]]},{"id":19103,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"Which statement about core vs peripheral temperature is correct?","o":["Core is monitored; fingers can be cooler","All parts are held at exactly 37 °C","Only finger temperature is monitored","Core temperature is not regulated","Temperature is one fixed number"],"e":"A neural mechanism constantly monitors <b>core temperature</b>; your fingers can be substantially cooler; temperature is a range.","w":["Range.","Core.","Regulated.","Range."],"k":"Core temperature monitored.","rn":[["","Which body temperature is monitored and controlled?","Which body temperature is monitored and controlled?","Core temperature; Not the fingers"],["","What monitors core temperature?","What monitors core temperature?","A neural mechanism"]]},{"id":19104,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"Which explains why Cl⁻ is imported via AE2 even though it is ultimately secreted into the lumen?","o":["Cl⁻ is needed for HCl and powers HCO₃⁻ export","Cl⁻ is converted into H⁺ inside the cell","Cl⁻ is stored permanently in the cell","Cl⁻ generates ATP for the pump","Cl⁻ blocks the proton pump"],"e":"More Cl⁻ outside; coupling Cl⁻ entry to HCO₃⁻ export removes bicarbonate and supplies Cl⁻, which then exits via CIC-2 to form HCl with H⁺.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"AE2 supplies Cl⁻ and removes HCO₃⁻."},{"id":19105,"t":"Fluid and Solutes of the Body","s":"In a 70 kg adult with 42 L of body fluid, approximately how much is plasma?","o":["About 2.8 L","About 10.4 L","About 0.8 L","About 14 L","About 28 L"],"e":"Total body fluid 42 L: intracellular 28 L (66.7%); extracellular 14 L (33.3%), made up of plasma 2.8 L (6.7%), interstitial fluid 10.4 L (24.7%) and transcellular fluid 0.8 L (1.9%).","w":["That is interstitial fluid.","That is transcellular fluid.","That is all extracellular fluid.","That is intracellular fluid."],"k":"Guyton & Hall ch 1, 4."},{"id":19106,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"A transporter moves a substance down its gradient using a protein but no ATP. If substrate concentration is increased tenfold beyond saturation, what happens to the rate?","o":["It stays at Vmax","It increases tenfold","It falls to zero","It reverses direction","It becomes active transport"],"e":"At saturation, rate = <b>Vmax</b>; more substrate cannot increase it.","w":["Simple diffusion would.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Beyond saturation: Vmax.","rn":[["","Vmax","Vmax","Maximum rate; Carrier saturated with target"]]},{"id":19107,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"Which statement about the role of the hypothalamus/brain in homeostasis matches the baroreceptor example?","o":["A group of medullary neurons acts as controller","The heart acts as controller","The blood vessels act as sensors and controller","The kidney controls BP alone","No neural control exists"],"e":"The “brain medulla vasomotor centre just means a bunch of neurons that control this”, using receptor information.","w":["Effector.","Effectors.","Not.","Neural."],"k":"Medulla neurons as controller."},{"id":19108,"t":"Fluid and Solutes of the Body","s":"A blood sample shows bicarbonate ~29 mM and chloride ~116 mM. Are these intracellular or extracellular values?","o":["Extracellular (blood)","Intracellular (cytosol)","Gastric lumen","CSF only","Mitochondrial"],"e":"Blood values: Cl⁻ 116, HCO₃⁻ 29 – both high outside cells.","w":["Outside.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ extracellular.","rn":[["","Chloride concentration in blood?","Chloride concentration in blood?","116 mM"],["","Bicarbonate concentration in blood?","Bicarbonate concentration in blood?","29 mM"],["","Extracellular vs cytosol: which is less acidic?","Extracellular vs cytosol: which is less acidic?","Extracellular fluid"]]},{"id":19109,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"Which of these is a membrane-spanning protein used in primary active transport?","o":["Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase","Aquaporin","Glucose carrier","Na⁺-glucose symporter","AE2"],"e":"Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase (and H⁺/K⁺ ATPase) are primary active pumps.","w":["Passive channel.","Passive carrier.","2° active.","2° active."],"k":"Na-K ATPase = 1° active.","rn":[["","What transports H+ into the gland lumen?","What transports H+ into the gland lumen?","H+/K+ ATPase pump; Gastric proton pump"],["","How does omeprazole inhibit the proton pump?","How does omeprazole inhibit the proton pump?","Disulphide bond; To H+/K+ ATPase residues"]]},{"id":19110,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Which statement best summarises what “passive” means in transport?","o":["Movement down a gradient without ATP","Movement down a gradient using ATP","Movement against a gradient without ATP","Movement of water only","Movement only through lipids"],"e":"Passive transport is always related to diffusion down a concentration gradient – no ATP.","w":["No ATP.","Active.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Passive = down gradient, no ATP.","rn":[["","Passive Transport","Passive Transport","Movement down the concentration gradient; No help, no energy needed"],["","Diffusion","Diffusion","Molecules move down; Their concentration gradient"],["","Can molecules move up their gradient without help?","Can molecules move up their gradient without help?","No, never"]]},{"id":19112,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"Which statement about positive feedback loops structurally is correct?","o":["Controller → effector, with no sensing step returning the variable","Stimulus → sensing → controller → effector, returning the variable","Controller → effector → sensing, returning the variable","They always include baroreceptors","They return variables to the nominal range"],"e":"Positive feedback loop: just <b>controller → effector → controller</b>; no sensing step bringing the variable back.","w":["Negative.","No sensing.","Not.","Amplify."],"k":"Positive loop: controller ↔ effector.","rn":[["","What are the 4 steps of the negative feedback loop?","What are the 4 steps of the negative feedback loop?","Stimulus; Sensing; Controller; Effector"],["","What happens when a variable moves beyond nominal?","What happens when a variable moves beyond nominal?","Controller initiates negative feedback; Via the effector; Variable returns to normal range"],["","Positive feedback loop: what 2 parts?","Positive feedback loop: what 2 parts?","Controller; Effector"]]},{"id":19113,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"A drug raises extracellular K⁺ markedly. Which organ effect is most concerning?","o":["Cardiac arrhythmias","Crenation of red cells","Increased HCl secretion","Hypothermia","Increased sweating"],"e":"Hyperkalaemia: cardiac arrhythmias – losing electrical control of the heart.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"High K⁺ → arrhythmia.","rn":[["","What is a cardiac arrhythmia?","What is a cardiac arrhythmia?","Heart pumps irregularly; Loss of electrical control"],["","Hyperkalaemia: 2 signs?","Hyperkalaemia: 2 signs?","Muscle cramps; Cardiac arrhythmias"]]},{"id":19114,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"Name the gastric gland cells that secrete HCl.","e":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells with canaliculi secrete HCl (about 160 mM in the lumen).","k":"Parietal cells.","a":["parietal cells","parietal cell","oxyntic cells","oxyntic cell","parietal","oxyntic"],"rn":[["","What do parietal cells release?","What do parietal cells release?","HCl into the stomach"],["","What 3 structures are seen in a gastric gland?","What 3 structures are seen in a gastric gland?","Mucous neck cells; Oxyntic (parietal) cells; Canaliculi"],["","Parietal cells are also called what?","Parietal cells are also called what?","Oxyntic cells"]]},{"id":19115,"t":"Physiology and Pathophysiology","s":"What does pathophysiology look at in terms of levels?","o":["Tissue and molecular levels","The gross anatomical level only","Behaviour only","The whole body only","DNA only"],"e":"Pathophysiology looks at the tissue level and also the <b>molecular level of disease formation</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Pathophysiology levels.","rn":[["","At what levels does pathophysiology look at disease formation?","At what levels does pathophysiology look at disease formation?","Tissue level; Molecular level"]]},{"id":19116,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"Which explains “cheap and elegant” as a description of the symporter?","o":["Each Na⁺ passing changes its shape, without ATP","Each ATP used changes its shape to bring glucose in","It needs no protein to move glucose","It uses light energy to move glucose","It moves water with glucose"],"e":"“Every time one single sodium ion goes through here, it changes the shape.” Cheap and elegant – no direct ATP.","w":["No ATP.","Protein.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Symporter efficiency.","rn":[["","How does ATP drive the pump?","How does ATP drive the pump?","Hydrolysis releases energy; Changes protein shape"]]},{"id":19117,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"A patient has hypokalaemia. Which symptoms are listed for it?","o":["Fatigue and muscle twitching","Muscle cramps and cardiac arrhythmias","Tingling and numbness","Fast breathing and restlessness","Nausea and confusion"],"e":"Hypokalaemia: fatigue and muscle twitching. Hyperkalaemia: muscle cramps and cardiac arrhythmias. Hypernatraemia: fast breathing, restlessness. Hyponatraemia: neurological issues, headache. Hypocalcaemia: tingling/numbness. Hypercalcaemia: nausea, confusion.","w":["Hyperkalaemia.","Hypocalcaemia.","Hypernatraemia.","Hypercalcaemia."],"k":"Billman 2020.","rn":[["","Hypokalaemia: 2 signs?","Hypokalaemia: 2 signs?","Fatigue; Muscle twitching"]]},{"id":19119,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"What happens to arterial pressure and coronary flow after 2 L blood loss, and why does it matter for the heart?","o":["Both fall, so the heart gets less oxygen","Both rise, so the heart works harder and fails","Arterial pressure rises but coronary flow falls","Coronary flow rises but arterial pressure falls","Neither changes much"],"e":"Arterial pressure falls and coronary blood flow reduces → heart gets less oxygen/nutrients → pumps less → further fall.","w":["They fall.","Both fall.","Both fall.","They fall."],"k":"2 L loss: pressure and coronary flow fall.","rn":[["","Why does falling arterial pressure weaken the heart?","Why does falling arterial pressure weaken the heart?","Less oxygen, nutrients; So pumps less"]]},{"id":19121,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"A 65-year-old man stands up quickly from lying down and his blood pressure briefly falls. Stretch-sensing receptors in the walls of his carotid arteries and aortic arch become relaxed. Within seconds his heart rate rises and his blood vessels constrict, and his blood pressure returns to normal.<br><br>What type of control is this, and which structure acts as the controller?","o":["Negative feedback; the vasomotor centres of the brain medulla","Positive feedback; the baroreceptors themselves","Negative feedback; the heart, acting as the sensor","Positive feedback; the sympathetic nervous system, amplifying the fall in pressure until it stops","Negative feedback; the hypothalamus via sweating"],"e":"Step 1: baroreceptors in the carotid and aortic arch walls sense arterial pressure; when pressure falls they relax, increasing activity of the vasomotor centre, which causes vasoconstriction and increased heart rate via the sympathetic nervous system. Step 2: this returns the variable to the nominal range, so it is negative feedback, with the brain medulla vasomotor centres as the controller, baroreceptors as sensors and the heart and vessels as effectors.","w":["The response restores pressure, so it is negative feedback, and baroreceptors are the sensors.","The heart is an effector, not the sensor.","The response brings pressure back to normal rather than amplifying it.","Sweating is thermoregulation, not blood pressure control."],"k":"Baroreflex: negative feedback; controller = medulla vasomotor centres.","rn":[["","Baroreceptor loop: what is the controller?","Baroreceptor loop: what is the controller?","Brain medulla vasomotor centres"]]},{"id":19122,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"Two trauma patients lose blood. Patient A loses 1 L and, after initial treatment, his blood pressure recovers. Patient B loses 2 L; his arterial pressure falls, coronary blood flow drops, his heart weakens further, and his pressure keeps falling despite his body's responses.<br><br>Which statement best explains patient B's course?","o":["Homeostasis has failed and positive feedback amplifies the fall","Negative feedback has restored his pressure to the nominal range","Positive feedback is stabilising his circulation, as it does in birth","His baroreceptors have increased heart rate enough to restore coronary flow, so the fall in pressure will stop on its own","Osmosis has moved water into his cells, raising blood pressure"],"e":"Step 1: 1 L blood loss is recoverable by homeostatic control, but 2 L is potentially non-recoverable because there is too little blood for the heart to pump effectively, so arterial pressure and coronary blood flow fall. Step 2: this weakens the heart further, reducing flow further: a self-amplifying positive feedback cycle that causes instability and loss of equilibrium.","w":["His pressure keeps falling, so control has failed.","Positive feedback here amplifies the fall rather than stabilising it.","His pressure keeps falling despite his responses.","Osmosis is not the mechanism described."],"k":"2 L blood loss → positive feedback → failure of homeostasis.","rn":[["","Why does 2 L blood loss become a vicious cycle?","Why does 2 L blood loss become a vicious cycle?","Arterial pressure falls; Coronary flow reduces; Positive feedback amplifies"]]},{"id":19123,"t":"Homeostasis","s":"A woman is in labour. As the baby's head presses on the cervix, the cervix stretches and a hormone is released. The hormone makes the uterus contract more strongly, which pushes the head harder against the cervix, releasing more hormone. The cycle continues until the baby is born.<br><br>Which hormone and which type of control is this, and why is it appropriate here?","o":["Oxytocin; positive feedback, appropriate because the cycle should amplify until birth","Oxytocin; negative feedback that returns the cervix to normal","Adrenaline; positive feedback that stops contractions","Oxytocin; negative feedback, because the uterus contracts to keep the cervix within its nominal range","Insulin; positive feedback that raises blood glucose"],"e":"Step 1: stretching of the cervix causes release of oxytocin, which stimulates the uterus to contract, increasing pressure and stretching on the cervix, so the cycle amplifies. Step 2: positive feedback is self-perpetuating and usually causes instability, but it is appropriate under some circumstances, such as birth, where the process needs to build to a conclusion.","w":["The cycle amplifies, so it is positive feedback.","The lecture names oxytocin, and contractions increase.","The cycle amplifies stretch rather than keeping it in range.","Insulin is not part of this example."],"k":"Birth: cervix stretch → oxytocin → contraction: appropriate positive feedback.","rn":[["","Birth feedback: first 2 steps?","Birth feedback: first 2 steps?","Cervix stretch releases oxytocin; Oxytocin: uterus contracts"],["","Birth feedback: last 2 steps?","Birth feedback: last 2 steps?","More cervical pressure, stretch; Cycle amplifies"]]},{"id":19124,"t":"Diffusion and Osmosis","s":"A laboratory places red blood cells into three solutions. In solution X (280 mOsm/L) the cells look unchanged. In solution Y (200 mOsm/L) they swell and burst. In solution Z (360 mOsm/L) they shrink and develop a spiky outline.<br><br>Which description of the solutions and water movement is correct?","o":["X isotonic; Y hypotonic, water moves in → lysis; Z hypertonic, water moves out → crenation","X hypotonic; Y isotonic; Z hypertonic, water moves in → lysis","X isotonic; Y hypertonic, water moves out; Z hypotonic, water moves in","X hypertonic; Y hypotonic → crenation; Z isotonic → lysis","All three are isotonic, as osmosis does not occur across red blood cell membranes"],"e":"Step 1: in an isotonic solution (280 mOsm/L) there is no difference in water concentration, so cells do not change. Step 2: in a hypotonic solution (200 mOsm/L) water concentration outside is higher, so water moves in, the cell swells and lyses; in a hypertonic solution (360 mOsm/L) water concentration outside is lower, so water moves out and the cell shrinks (crenation).","w":["X causes no change, so it is isotonic.","Y causes swelling, so it is hypotonic.","Swelling and lysis are hypotonic; shrinking is hypertonic.","The cells change shape, so osmosis occurs."],"k":"Hypotonic → lysis; hypertonic → crenation; isotonic → no change.","rn":[["","Isotonic vs hypotonic vs hypertonic: osmolarity used","Isotonic vs hypotonic vs hypertonic: osmolarity used","Isotonic 280 mOsm/L; Hypotonic 200 mOsm/L; Hypertonic 360 mOsm/L"],["","What are the 3 types of solution a cell can be placed in?","What are the 3 types of solution a cell can be placed in?","Isotonic; Hypotonic; Hypertonic"]]},{"id":19125,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"A researcher measures glucose uptake into cells as she raises the outside glucose concentration. At low concentrations, uptake rises steeply. At high concentrations, the rate stops rising and levels off, even though more glucose is added. A second substance crossing the membrane directly shows uptake rising in a straight line.<br><br>How is glucose crossing, and why does the rate level off?","o":["Facilitated diffusion via a carrier; the carrier saturates at Vmax","Simple diffusion; the membrane becomes full of glucose","Primary active transport; the cells run out of ATP","Facilitated diffusion via a carrier; glucose starts moving against its gradient once the carrier is full","Osmosis; water stops moving into the cells"],"e":"Step 1: carrier proteins change conformation after binding their target and allow membrane passage (facilitated diffusion), while simple diffusion shows a straight-line increase with concentration. Step 2: at high concentration, the rate of facilitated diffusion reaches a maximum as the carrier becomes saturated with its target molecule, known as the Vmax.","w":["Simple diffusion does not saturate; it rises in a straight line.","Facilitated glucose transport does not use ATP.","Facilitated diffusion moves with the gradient.","Osmosis is water movement, not glucose uptake."],"k":"Facilitated diffusion saturates at Vmax; simple diffusion is linear.","rn":[["","Vmax","Vmax","Maximum rate; Carrier saturated with target"],["","Why does facilitated diffusion plateau?","Why does facilitated diffusion plateau?","Carrier proteins become saturated"],["","Is facilitated diffusion via a carrier still passive?","Is facilitated diffusion via a carrier still passive?","Yes, no ATP"]]},{"id":19126,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"An intestinal cell takes up glucose even though the glucose concentration inside the cell is higher than outside. Uptake stops when a drug abolishes the sodium gradient across the membrane, although the cell still has plenty of ATP. The transporter moves sodium and glucose in the same direction.<br><br>What type of transport is this?","o":["Secondary active transport by a symporter using the Na⁺ gradient","Primary active transport using ATP directly","Facilitated diffusion down the glucose gradient","Secondary active transport by an antiporter moving Na⁺ and glucose in opposite directions across the membrane","Simple diffusion through the bilayer"],"e":"Step 1: glucose moves against its gradient, which needs energy, but the transporter does not use ATP directly; it depends on the Na⁺ gradient. Step 2: in secondary active transport via a symporter, Na⁺ moves down its concentration gradient into the cell, allowing glucose to pass against its gradient in the same direction.","w":["ATP is present but uptake depends on the Na⁺ gradient.","Glucose is moving against its gradient.","The two move in the same direction, so it is a symporter.","Simple diffusion cannot move glucose against its gradient."],"k":"Na⁺–glucose symporter: 2° active, same direction, uses Na⁺ gradient.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of transporter is this? How does it work?","What type of transporter is this? How does it work?","Symporter (secondary active); Na+ down its gradient; Glucose against, same direction"],["","Why is the symporter \"secondary\" active?","Why is the symporter \"secondary\" active?","Uses Na+ gradient; Not ATP"],["","Symporters and antiporters: transport type?","Symporters and antiporters: transport type?","Secondary active transport"]]},{"id":19127,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"A 50-year-old woman with severe heartburn is prescribed omeprazole. Her stomach acid output falls markedly. Her doctor explains that her stomach's parietal cells normally make concentrated acid using an enzyme that combines CO₂ and water, a pump on the lumen side, and an exchanger on the blood side.<br><br>Which step does omeprazole block, and what happens to the bicarbonate the cell makes?","o":["The H⁺/K⁺ ATPase; HCO₃⁻ normally leaves to plasma via AE2 in exchange for Cl⁻","Carbonic anhydrase; HCO₃⁻ is pumped into the lumen","AE2; HCO₃⁻ is secreted into the lumen with H⁺","The CIC-2 channel; HCO₃⁻ is recycled with K⁺ through the KCNJ15 channel into the stomach lumen","The aquaporin; HCO₃⁻ diffuses into the lumen"],"e":"Step 1: in the parietal cell, carbonic anhydrase converts H₂O + CO₂ to H₂CO₃, which gives HCO₃⁻ + H⁺, and H⁺ is pumped into the lumen by the H⁺/K⁺ ATPase (gastric proton pump); omeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor that forms a disulphide bond with this pump. Step 2: HCO₃⁻ is transported to the plasma via the Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ anion exchanger 2 (AE2), bringing Cl⁻ in, which then leaves to the lumen via CIC-2 to form HCl (~160 mM).","w":["Omeprazole blocks the proton pump, and HCO₃⁻ goes to the plasma.","Oxonol dyes, not omeprazole, inhibit AE2.","CIC-2 carries Cl⁻, and KCNJ15 recycles K⁺.","Omeprazole acts on the proton pump."],"k":"Omeprazole blocks H⁺/K⁺ ATPase; HCO₃⁻ exits via AE2 for Cl⁻.","rn":[["","Plasma pH is regulated via which 2 organs?","Plasma pH is regulated via which 2 organs?","Lungs; Kidneys"]]},{"id":19128,"t":"Membrane Transport","s":"A 70-year-old man with kidney problems develops muscle cramps, and his heart monitor shows an irregular rhythm. Blood tests show that one ion, normally present at about 4 mM in blood, is well above normal. His sodium and calcium are normal.<br><br>Which imbalance is this, and what features would the opposite imbalance cause?","o":["Hyperkalaemia; hypokalaemia causes fatigue and muscle twitching","Hypernatraemia; hyponatraemia causes headache and confusion","Hypercalcaemia; hypocalcaemia causes tingling and numbness","Hypokalaemia; hyperkalaemia causes fatigue","Hyperkalaemia; hypokalaemia causes fast breathing and restlessness"],"e":"Step 1: potassium is about 4 mM in blood, and too much (hyperkalaemia) causes muscle cramps and cardiac arrhythmias. Step 2: too little potassium (hypokalaemia) causes fatigue and muscle twitching.","w":["His sodium is normal; sodium is about 145 mM.","His calcium is normal.","High, not low, potassium fits his cramps and arrhythmia.","Fast breathing and restlessness are features of hypernatraemia."],"k":"Hyperkalaemia: cramps, arrhythmias; hypokalaemia: fatigue, twitching.","rn":[["","Hypokalaemia: 2 signs?","Hypokalaemia: 2 signs?","Fatigue; Muscle twitching"]]},{"id":19129,"t":"pH and the Stomach","s":"A physiology student follows CO₂ through the body. In a working muscle, CO₂ and water combine to form carbonic acid, which gives bicarbonate and H⁺. When the blood reaches the lungs, the reverse reaction occurs and CO₂ is breathed out.<br><br>Which enzyme catalyses both reactions, and what decides the direction?","o":["Carbonic anhydrase; the direction is driven by pH and the environment","H⁺/K⁺ ATPase; the direction is driven by ATP supply","Carbonic anhydrase; it works only in one direction, in the lungs","AE2; it swaps HCO₃⁻ for Cl⁻ in the lungs, which converts it back into CO₂ and water","Aquaporin; the direction is driven by water concentration"],"e":"Step 1: carbonic anhydrase catalyses H₂O + CO₂ ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ HCO₃⁻ + H⁺, and the reaction is reversible. Step 2: in other tissues the reaction runs H₂O + CO₂ → HCO₃⁻ + H⁺, while in the lungs it runs HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ → H₂O + CO₂; the direction depends on the environment and is driven by pH.","w":["The proton pump moves H⁺; it does not convert CO₂.","The reaction is reversible and runs both ways.","AE2 is an exchanger, not the enzyme that converts HCO₃⁻.","Aquaporins are water channels."],"k":"Carbonic anhydrase is reversible; direction driven by pH.","rn":[["","What decides the direction of the carbonic anhydrase reaction?","What decides the direction of the carbonic anhydrase reaction?","The environment / pH"]]},{"id":19130,"t":"Electrochemical Gradients","s":"A cell has a membrane potential that is negative inside and positive outside. Both Na⁺ and Cl⁻ are more concentrated outside the cell than inside. When channels for each ion open, Na⁺ rushes in strongly while Cl⁻ enters only a little.<br><br>Which explanation is correct?","o":["For Na⁺, chemical and electrical forces both push inward; for Cl⁻, voltage opposes the chemical push","Both are pushed in equally by concentration alone","Na⁺ enters by primary active transport; Cl⁻ by osmosis","For Cl⁻ both forces push it in; for Na⁺ the voltage opposes it","Na⁺ enters because the membrane is normally freely permeable to charged ions"],"e":"Step 1: the electrochemical gradient has a chemical (concentration) part and an electrical (charge) part. Step 2: with the inside negative, the positive Na⁺ ion has a large inward driving force because both concentration and voltage push it in, while the negative Cl⁻ ion has only a small inward force because voltage opposes it.","w":["Voltage also acts, so the two ions differ.","Opening channels allows passive movement, not active transport or osmosis.","The forces are the other way round.","Membranes are not normally permeable to charged ions."],"k":"Electrochemical gradient = concentration + voltage; inside-negative favours cation entry.","rn":[["","Summary: the distribution of ions is a consequence of what?","Summary: the distribution of ions is a consequence of what?","Membrane properties; Electrical and chemical forces"]]}]);
